WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:10.400
Hey Wisecrack, Jared again.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.920
Today, we’re talking about an insanely popular Halloween horror series guaranteed to make

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:17.250
you barf up all your candy corn.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:18.710
No, not "The Emoji Movie."

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:20.270
We're talking "Saw."

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:24.850
Since 2004, the torture-porny franchise has been locking up morally-dubious characters

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:29.920
in all kinds of gruesome, steel contraptions and inventing creative, new places to hide

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:30.920
the keys.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.529
“It’s right before your eyes.”

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:37.780
The creepy puppet-master of the Saw franchise is Jigsaw, and boy, does he loves playing games!

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:42.740
And by playing games, we mean locking unconscious people inside dank warehouses and giving them

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:47.240
60 seconds to choose between certain death, self-mutilation, or, if they’re lucky,

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.140
disemboweling a stranger.

00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:53.750
When people talk about “Saw,” they tend to focus on Jigsaw’s impressive body count.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.400
But just as you can pick through a pit of heroin syringes to find the hidden key,

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.960
you can sift through this villain’s deadly traps to find the grander meaning of his games.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:05.080
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy of Saw.

00:01:05.089 --> 00:01:07.540
And, as always: spoilers ahead

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:13.140
The Philosophy of Saw all comes down to Jigsaw’s motivation: his torturous games are opportunities

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:18.860
for his victims to repent for their sins and ostensibly, to transform into better human beings.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:26.820
If they survive his tests, (and the odds are not quite ever in their favor),

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:30.680
he believes that they’ll emerge with a newfound ability to appreciate life.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.440
“Most people are so ungrateful to be alive.

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:35.560
But not you.”

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:38.280
So basically, if Tony Robbins had a baby with Satan.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:44.140
In a sense, Jigsaw embodies a kind of extremist Christian morality, but before we get there,

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.680
we first need to get in the headspace of John Kramer, alias, the Jigsaw Killer.

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:52.979
Once upon a time, John was a perfectly nice civil engineer who loved hanging out with

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:56.210
his pregnant wife, Jill, who owned a drug rehab clinic.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:00.710
Then, one of Jill’s tweaked-out patients, Cecil Adams, robs her clinic, accidentally

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.580
injuring Jill in the process.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.899
She suffers a miscarriage, and John falls into a deep depression.

00:02:05.899 --> 00:02:10.429
A short while after, he’s diagnosed with a brain tumor and terminal cancer.

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:15.069
He plummets further into depression, and his relationship with Jill quickly deteriorates.

00:02:15.069 --> 00:02:16.860
“Let’s review.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.040
Girl loves boy, boy loves girl.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:23.000
Boy gets girl pregnant, girl loses baby…

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.060
Boy turns into a serial killer.”

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:31.100
And instead of joining Tinder or something, Jill continues to entangle herself in her boo’s drama.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:36.040
After an all-around sh*t year, John tries to kill himself by driving off a cliff.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:40.740
Miraculously, he survives the crash and emerges, totally sure of his new calling:

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.560
dispensing morality, one torture device at a time.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:57.430
As John cooked up a fresh code of morality to match his new mission in life, he seems

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:02.030
to have been inspired by an unlikely source: early Christian philosophy.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.100
Specifically, its views on suffering.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:08.840
According to Early Christian tradition, God is good, and everything he created is good.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.060
And nobody really questioned that until one day, an Early Christian stubbed his toe and

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.960
discovered suffering and was like, “This sucks!

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.240
WTF, GOD!”

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.160
Once a bunch of Christian theologians started stubbing their toes, they realized that they

00:03:21.162 --> 00:03:25.570
needed to rationalize how an all-good God could have created such suffering.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:29.690
They theorized that suffering had to be the fault of no-good, lousy humans.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:33.240
Here’s what they figured: When Adam ate that apple from the Tree of Knowledge,

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:38.880
thus committing the Original Sin, he separated dumb, evil humans from the pure, perfect God,

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.720
who apparently doesn’t like it when humans eat a fresh, healthy snack.

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:48.140
From then on, sinful humans would need to suffer and repent if they wanted to be reunited with God.

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:53.240
Suffering then, is the result of our own sins, and an expression of the pain we feel over

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:54.960
our separation from God.

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.800
In this way, traditional Christianity justified suffering as the fault of the sinner, not of God.

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:02.140
Crisis averted!

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:06.710
Similarly, Jigsaw thinks his victims deserve the suffering that they endure in his traps.

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:11.940
He helpfully reminds each victim of the sins they’ve committed to get there, and he doesn’t hold back.

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:18.079
“For years you have burned those around you with your lies, cons, and deceits.”

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:23.520
As Jigsaw sees it, his victims are enslaved by their past immorality, not his industrial-strength

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:27.960
torture devices — much like theologians believed humanity has been trapped by Adam

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.450
and Eve’s original sin.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:34.380
Fittingly, the Saw series has a few Adams of its own: In Saw IV, we learn that drug

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:38.000
addict Cecil Adams is responsible for Jill’s miscarriage.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:42.320
His original sin is a catalyst for John’s transformation into Jigsaw.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:47.030
Additionally, in the first film, Adam Stanheight, a photographer who spends his days spying

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:49.500
on people, becomes one of Jigsaw’s targets.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:55.160
His professional quest for seedy “knowledge” mirrors that of Adam’s desire to eat from the Tree.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:05:00.020
So Kramer’s view on human suffering shares a similar origin story to that of Early Christians,

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:04.130
which, hey, could mean that watching a “Saw” movie qualifies as attending church.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:08.420
However, John Kramer also shares a common thread with Christian theorist and father

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:13.280
of Existentialism, Soren Kierkegaard, who sees suffering as a critical part of personal

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:15.210
and spiritual growth.

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:18.970
For context: much like Kramer, Kierkegaard had his share of sh*tty luck.

00:05:18.970 --> 00:05:24.351
He had a lifelong physical deformity, watched his mother, brothers and sisters die at a

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:28.680
young age, and became totally convinced that he was going to die at age 33.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:33.100
Like Kramer he also broke up with the love of his life, his then-fiancee Regine, yet

00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:35.790
continued to pine after her for the rest of his days.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:39.090
Basically, the two of them could have shared stories over whiskey.

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:44.080
They also both believed that suffering purifies the sufferer, teaches them to appreciate life,

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:47.160
and forces them to take responsibility for their actions.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.200
You might prefer your life without a generous dose of suffering, but hear the guy out:

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:56.400
Kierkegaard believed that an individual must pass through three distinct phases to become his or her

00:05:56.400 --> 00:06:01.340
“true self:” First: ”The aesthetic,” driven by sensual pleasures and lacking any morality;

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:06.100
Second; “the ethical,” driven by morality and belief in individual responsibility,

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:09.480
and third; “the religious,” driven by faith in God.

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:14.480
So, how do you go from being a lowly drunk “aesthete,” to an upstanding, “ethical” guy?

00:06:14.480 --> 00:06:17.520
You could always try out one of Jigsaw’s torture machines!

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.169
That’s right: according to Kierkegaard, suffering is key to moving up to the next

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:23.910
stage of human development.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:28.500
Kierkegaard was mostly talking about spiritual and existential suffering, not being caught

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:33.410
in a torture device, or worse, having people spoil “Game of Thrones.”

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:38.220
Still, Jigsaw’s machines can be understood as a test for each of his victims, who are

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:42.200
typically aesthetes driven by desires for sex, drugs, or money.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:46.710
His tests present his victims, with an ethical quandary that usually forces them to make

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.140
a painful decision.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:52.920
This matches the way Kierkegaard explains the journey from aesthetic to ethical being:

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:07.620
But how exactly does suffering help you become more ethical?

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:11.940
According to Kierkegaard, suffering forces a human to reflect on their situation,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.760
if only to think, “Oh, f**k this sucks.”

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:19.600
That moment of reflection summons “the first doubt,” which Kierkegaard describes as the thought,

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:22.440
“Why is this happening to me; can it not be otherwise?”

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.020
Or to put it more simply: “What the f**k is going on?

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:26.020
Where am I?”

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:27.020
“What is this?”

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:28.020
“What is this?”

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:30.880
That little WTF? moment

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:34.000
generates the immediate desire to change your circumstance.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.320
“Help!”

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:36.360
“Help!”

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:37.400
“Help!”

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:42.120
This desire opens a person up to what Kierkegaard calls “ethical self-understanding.”

00:07:42.120 --> 00:07:47.460
Suffering is growth-inducing because it “turns the single individual over to himself completely.”

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:51.880
At its most successful, this suffering can force a person to repent for past sins,

00:07:51.880 --> 00:07:56.000
take responsibility for his guilt, and choose to seek his own survival.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.480
But more on that later.

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:02.350
As you progress up the ladder of development, a piece of your old, “false self,” dies,

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:03.610
much like the old Taylor Swift.

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:06.550
“The old Taylor can’t come to phone right now…

00:08:06.550 --> 00:08:07.550
‘Cause she’s dead!”

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:11.510
Judging from his own interpretation of his car crash, John Kramer agrees: “A different

00:08:11.510 --> 00:08:13.469
person crawled out through the wreckage.”

00:08:13.469 --> 00:08:18.570
For Kierkegaard, the ultimate goal of spiritual suffering is to completely kill off your flawed,

00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:22.950
false self in order to make room for your recreation into a better, truer self.

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:28.540
Kramer demands this of his most fervent follower, Amanda, when she agrees to live by his ethical code.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.300
“You will give everything to me.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:31.560
Every cell in your body.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:32.620
Is that understood?”

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:33.340
“Yes.”

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:35.039
“You leave that life behind.

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:40.120
When you go down that corridor, there is no turning back, do you understand that?”

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:43.599
Amanda sits in a prayer-like position, listening deeply.

00:08:43.599 --> 00:08:48.180
Die-hard Saw fans call this scene “Amanda’s baptism,” which is fitting when you consider

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:50.430
how baptism relates to suffering.

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:55.899
Just as Jesus rose from the dead to redeem humanity, human beings are baptised and reborn,

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:57.380
“spiritually cleansed.”

00:08:57.380 --> 00:09:01.399
Christian tradition also see suffering as a mechanism for “spiritual cleansing.”

00:09:01.399 --> 00:09:05.279
Jigsaw frequently references this concept of rebirth, whether he’s chatting with his

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:06.480
favorite disciple —

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:12.520
– or welcoming a new victim into one of
his traps —

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:22.700
Jigsaw even loses his “kidnapping” virginity during a literal celebration of rebirth:

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:24.580
The Chinese New Year celebration.

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:29.740
Because it’s the year of the Pig, Kramer even picks up his sweet trademark pig mask at the event.

00:09:29.740 --> 00:09:33.000
Kierkegaard and Jigsaw have a few other important ideas in common.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.060
Specifically, they both value personal responsibility.

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:40.589
Kierkegaard writes that the first indispensable step of becoming “a real man” isn’t

00:09:40.589 --> 00:09:45.430
getting strange new body hair, but by becoming a “responsible individual before God.”

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:47.780
Kramer demands the same from his victims:

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:55.140
And what’s the key to taking responsibility?

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:56.660
Making a choice.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.600
Kramer spurs his victims into life-affirming action.

00:10:11.620 --> 00:10:16.300
In the moment of making a choice, Kierkegaard believed, a man “becomes himself.”

00:10:16.310 --> 00:10:20.490
Similarly, Kramer is adamant that his victims “choose” their actions, even ending each

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:23.000
of his games with this yoda-like refrain:

00:10:31.059 --> 00:10:35.379
He also maintains that the individual is responsible for the outcome of their decision within the

00:10:35.379 --> 00:10:36.730
confines of his game.

00:10:36.730 --> 00:10:39.240
“I’ve never murdered anyone in my life.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:40.990
The decisions are up to them.”

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:45.959
While many horror films offer an ultimate salvation — “You’re dead, Krueger!”

00:10:45.959 --> 00:10:49.790
— Saw does not offer any kind of hope for a better tomorrow.

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:54.660
Instead, it seems to critique this extreme version of traditional Christian morality.

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:59.550
Throughout the series, Kramer is positioned as a god-like figure, monk, or even martyr.

00:10:59.550 --> 00:11:05.110
He dresses in a long, black hooded cape, acquires flocks of disciples, performs “baptisms.”

00:11:05.110 --> 00:11:09.119
He also maintains a quality of omniscience
 — he’s always watching his victims through

00:11:09.119 --> 00:11:10.819
camera monitors or peepholes.

00:11:10.819 --> 00:11:16.600
So, Jigsaw may be a God-like figure, but he’s a seriously failed one — his attempt to

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:21.980
establish a moral order does not bring about the meaningful change that Kramer professes it will.

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:26.520
For starters, most of his victims fail their tests — they crumble under pressure, or

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:27.980
can’t withstand the pain.

00:11:27.980 --> 00:11:30.840
Those who do pass are frequently traumatized.

00:11:30.860 --> 00:11:32.440
“Bunch of bullsh*t!

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:38.480
You wanna know the best thing that happened to me after having to cut off my own arm?

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:43.280
It’s handicapped parking at the damn mall!”

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:48.840
Further revealing the full extent of Jigsaw’s failure is his ill-fated quest for the right apprentice.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:51.700
Each of his contenders twist his philosophy.

00:11:51.709 --> 00:11:55.290
While Jigsaw creates games that are truly winnable — if you follow the rules,

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:58.780
you can choose to preserve your life — as long as you’re willing to part with, say,

00:11:58.780 --> 00:11:59.760
your left arm.

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:04.160
His mentees, on the other hand, create rigged games that are unwinnable death traps.

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:07.560
“If the aim of Jigsaw’s game was to get out before the bomb went off, then why was

00:12:07.560 --> 00:12:08.960
the door welded shut?”

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:12.519
His disciples just use the games to get revenge on people they don’t like.

00:12:12.519 --> 00:12:16.179
Eventually, they all end up trapping and killing one another out of jealousy.

00:12:16.179 --> 00:12:19.779
To them, the point of the games is punishment, rather than renewal.

00:12:19.779 --> 00:12:21.569
So much for Jigsaw’s legacy.

00:12:21.569 --> 00:12:25.490
The characters in the Saw films frequently remind the audience that this is a seriously

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:26.579
flawed moral code.

00:12:26.579 --> 00:12:28.129
“You said you wanted to talk.

00:12:28.129 --> 00:12:29.800
Then, you said you wanted to play a game.

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:31.700
You’re talking, but it means nothing!”

00:12:31.700 --> 00:12:33.800
Just before her death, Amanda denounces him:

00:12:38.660 --> 00:12:42.980
Lastly, because Jigsaw is presented as a God-like figure, you would expect that his justice

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:45.329
be dispensed fairly - but it’s not.

00:12:45.329 --> 00:12:50.760
He’s the villain of the series, and his morality lessons frequently sacrifice innocent human lives.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:54.700
Similarly, you’d expect his death to result in salvation or redemption, but instead,

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:58.660
he dies after offering one of his victims, Jeff, the opportunity to forgive him.

00:12:58.700 --> 00:12:59.820
"I forgive you."

00:12:59.820 --> 00:13:02.340
Instead, Jeff violently kills Kramer.

00:13:02.340 --> 00:13:06.560
Even Kramer’s own death is the result of one of his test’s ultimate failures.

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:09.760
And he didn’t even get to eat a tasty, delicious apple.

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:13.160
So much for finding spiritual rebirth in a reverse bear trap!

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:17.600
Still, plenty of us seem to find value in less intensive experiences of suffering —

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:20.920
just look at marathon runners or competitive hot dog eaters!

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:24.519
So when it comes to self-improvement, is it “no pain, no gain”?

00:13:24.519 --> 00:13:28.829
Or will human suffering be eliminated once we invent the perfect, dual-purpose food delivery

00:13:28.829 --> 00:13:30.360
and TV-streaming app?

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:31.939
Let us know what you think in the comments!

